CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters mandated under the Act on the Use of Electronic Documents in Criminal Justice Procedures and matters necessary for the implementation thereof.

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Decree are defined as follows:
  1. "Criminal justice portal" means the criminal justice portal defined in subparagraph 6 of Article 2 of the Act on Promotion of the Digitalization of the Criminal Justice Process, which is established to conduct criminal justice procedures using electronic documents;
  2. "Electronic records" means a compilation of electronic documents or digitized documents, organized on a case-by-case basis;
  3. "Investigative agency, etc." means a criminal justice agency, excluding a court;
  4. "Information and communications network" means an information and communications system for collecting, processing, storing, searching for, transmitting, or receiving information by using telecommunications facilities defined in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Framework Act on Telecommunications, or by utilizing such telecommunications facilities in combination with computers and computer-use technologies.

Article 3 (Persons eligible to submit electronic documents) Pursuant to Article 5(1) of the Act on the Use of Electronic Documents in Criminal Justice Procedures (hereinafter referred to as the "Act"), persons eligible to submit electronic documents through an electronic data processing system, in addition to suspects, defendants, victims, complainants, accusers, and defense counsels, shall be as follows:
  1. A legal representative of a suspect, defendant, victim, complainant, or accuser (including the spouse, lineal blood relatives, and siblings of the victim if the victim is deceased);
  2. The representative of a suspect, defendant, victim, complainant, or accuser, if such person is a corporation;
  3. A special representative under Article 28 of the Criminal Procedure Act;
  4. An assistant under Article 29 of the Criminal Procedure Act;
  5. A person who intends to become a defense counsel under Article 34 of the Criminal Procedure Act;
  6. An agent of a victim or complainant (including the following persons):
  a. An attorney-at-law appointed by the victim or complainant, or by the legal representative of the victim or complainant;
  b. A public defender selected by a prosecutor for a victim under relevant statutes or regulations, such as Article 27(6) of the Act on Special Cases concerning the Punishment of Sexual Crimes;
  7. A person in a relationship of trust with a suspect under Article 244-5 of the Criminal Procedure Act, a person in a relationship of trust with a defendant under Article 276-2 of that Act, and a person in a relationship of trust with a victim under Article 163-2 of that Act;
  8. An assistant to a victim under relevant statutes or regulations, such as Article 39-8 of the Welfare of Senior Citizens Act, Article 21 of the Child Welfare Act, or Article 59-8 of the Act on Welfare of Persons with Disabilities;
  9. An intermediary who aids a victim of sexual crimes, child abuse crimes, or abuse crimes against persons with disabilities in criminal justice procedures, etc. under relevant statutes or regulations, such as Article 35 of the Act on Special Cases concerning the Punishment of Sexual Crimes (including cases applied mutatis mutandis in Article 17 of the Act on Special Cases concerning the Punishment of Child Abuse Crimes) or Article 59-16 of the Act on Welfare of Persons with Disabilities;
  10. A professional investigative adviser under Article 245-2 of the Criminal Procedure Act;
  11. A psychiatrist, psychologist, social welfare scholar, or other relevant expert whose opinion has been requested by an investigative agency under Article 33(4) of the Act on Special Cases concerning the Punishment of Sexual Crimes;
  12. Interpreters, translators, and expert witnesses;
  13. An agency commissioned by an investigative agency, etc. to perform an expert examination;
  14. An owner, possessor, custodian, or submitter of seized articles (limited to cases where he or she claims the return or provisional return of the seized articles);
  15. A person for reference;
  16. A person under confidential probe or a person under suspicion;
  17. A petitioner or a respondent to a petition;
  18. A public office or other public or private organization requested to report necessary matters under Article 199(2) of the Criminal Procedure Act;
  19. A person subject to seizure who has received a transmission of a warrant, permit, written permission, request, etc. under Article 17 of the Act;
  20. A person who was a defendant in a case where a judgment of acquittal has become final and conclusive;
  21. A probationer under Article 3(1) of the Act on Probation and a subject of a community service order or lecture attendance order under paragraph (2) of that Article;
  22. Any of the following persons who have legitimate authority or duty to submit documents in relation to the business affairs under the jurisdiction of a probation office under Article 15 of the Act on Probation:
  a. A physician;
  b. A social worker;
  c. A mental health professional under Article 17 of the Act on the Improvement of Mental Health and the Support for Welfare Services for Mental Patients;
  d. A volunteer crime prevention commissioner under Article 18 of the Act on Probation;
  23. A guardian, etc. under Article 7(3) of the Act on the Treatment of Protected Juveniles, and a person who has legitimate authority or duty to submit documents in relation to the business affairs under the jurisdiction of a juvenile reformatory or the Juvenile Classification Review Center under Article 4 of that Act;
  24. A juvenile in a juvenile protection case under the Juvenile Act, and the legal representative or assistant of such juvenile;
  25. Other persons who have legitimate authority or duty to submit documents in accordance with criminal justice procedures under the statutes listed in the subparagraphs of Article 3 of the Act.

CHAPTER II USER REGISTRATION

Article 4 (User registration) (1) A person who intends to be registered as a user under Article 7(1) of the Act shall access the criminal justice portal, enter the necessary information for each of the following categories in the corresponding fields, and then apply for user registration under that paragraph (hereinafter referred to as "user registration") using a certificate for electronic signature (referring to a certificate that can verify the real name of the signer, designated by the Minister of Justice and publicly announced on the criminal justice portal; hereinafter the same shall apply); in such cases, the registration information of a person who has completed user registration (hereinafter referred to as "registered user") shall match the contents of the certificate:
  1. An individual member;
  2. A corporate member;
  3. A member of the Bar Association.
  (2) A registered user referred to in paragraph (1)2 and 3 may designate a user affiliated with him or her with a specified scope of authority for use, and the person so designated may access the criminal justice portal and file for registration as an affiliated user.
  (3) Investigative agencies, etc. may recommend that the following persons file for registration as users in a case where case records are managed as electronic records (hereinafter referred to as "electronic record case"):
  1. A suspect, defendant, victim, complainant, accuser, or defense counsel;
  2. Persons under the subparagraphs of Article 3;
  3. Persons not registered as users in an electronic record case.

Article 5 (Withdrawal of user registration) (1) A registered user who intends to withdraw user registration under Article 7(2) of the Act may withdraw user registration by accessing the criminal justice portal and entering the intention to withdraw user registration.
  (2) Even if user registration is withdrawn under paragraph (1), it shall not affect the validity of documents submitted by the registered user before the withdrawal or the validity of service or notification made electronically by investigative agencies, etc. to the registered user through the electronic data processing system.

Article 6 (Cancellation of user registration) (1) The head of an investigative agency, etc. may suspend the use of the electronic data processing system or cancel the registration of an affiliated user registered under Article 4(2) (hereinafter referred to as "affiliated user") in addition to a registered user under Article 7(3) of the Act.
  (2) The grounds for which the head of an investigative agency, etc. may suspend the use of the electronic data processing system by a registered user (including an affiliated user; hereinafter the same shall apply) or cancel user registration (including the registration of an affiliated user under paragraph (1); hereinafter the same shall apply) under Article 7(3)5 of the Act shall be as follows:
  1. Where the user registration is used for a purpose other than its original purpose, such as for the purpose of delaying criminal justice procedures;
  2. Where the registered user lacks mental capacity;
  3. Where 5 years have passed since the last day the registered user submitted or inspected an electronic document using the criminal justice portal;
  4. Other cases where it is necessary to suspend the use of the electronic data processing system or cancel user registration, which are equivalent to Article 7(3)1 through 4 of the Act or subparagraphs 1 through 3 of this paragraph.
  (3) If the head of an investigative agency, etc. deems it necessary to determine whether the grounds under the subparagraphs (excluding subparagraph 3) of Article 7(3) of the Act apply, he or she may temporarily suspend the use by the relevant registered user until a decision on cancellation is made. In such cases, the head of an investigative agency, etc. shall notify the registered user of such fact via email, text message transmission service, etc.
  (4) The head of an investigative agency, etc. shall notify the relevant registered user of the grounds in advance via email, text message transmission service, etc. and grant him or her an opportunity to present an explanation before canceling user registration under Article 7(3) of the Act (excluding cases of cancellation on the grounds provided in paragraph (2)3 of this Article).

CHAPTER III SUBMISSION AND RECEIPT OF ELECTRONIC DOCUMENTS

Article 7 (Electronic signatures) (1) Except as otherwise provided in this Decree, a person who intends to submit electronic documents to investigative agencies, etc. under Article 8(1) of the Act shall affix an administrative electronic signature or an electronic signature using a certificate to the electronic documents.
  (2) If a person who cannot use an electronic signature (limited to those that can verify the real name of the signer) under the main clause of Article 8(1) of the Act intends to submit electronic documents, he or she may affix an electronic signature by means of an information processing device, such as a computer, after having his or her identity verified by the relevant investigative agency, etc.
  (3) If a person who cannot use an electronic signature under the main clause of Article 8(1) of the Act (limited to those that can verify the real name of the signer) intends to submit electronic documents, he or she may submit electronic documents by submitting an information storage medium containing the electronic documents after having his or her identity verified by the relevant investigative agency, etc. In such cases, a public official affiliated with the investigative agency, etc. that received the electronic documents shall register the electronic documents in the electronic data processing system after affixing an administrative electronic signature to the received electronic documents.
  (4) In cases of preparing a written decision, protocol, report, etc. as an electronic document under Article 8(3) of the Act, a public official affiliated with an investigative agency, etc. shall save it in the electronic data processing system after affixing an administrative electronic signature using a certificate for administrative electronic signature under Article 30 of the Enforcement Decree of the Electronic Government Act. In such cases, if the signature of a person making a statement is required in criminal justice procedures under the statutes listed in the subparagraphs of Article 3 of the Act, the public official shall have the person making a statement whose identity has been verified affix an electronic signature to the protocol, etc.

Article 8 (Methods and procedures for receiving electronic documents) (1) The Minister of Justice shall designate the file format, composition method, and other matters regarding electronic documents that can be submitted through the electronic data processing system under the former part of Article 9(2) of the Act, and publicly announce them on the criminal justice portal.
  (2) A registered user shall not combine arguments, evidence, or other matters into a single file for submission, but shall submit them as separate files in accordance with the method required by the criminal justice portal.
  (3) A registered user shall submit electronic documents in a format that allows for the search and extraction of text information, unless there are unavoidable circumstances.

Article 9 (Submission of electronic documents) (1) A registered user may prepare or submit documents as electronic documents by entering relevant matters or registering electronic documents in the manner required by the criminal justice portal.
  (2) Where multiple registered users share a common interest, they may submit a single electronic document under joint names by any of the following methods:
  1. Submitting the electronic document after all joint submitters have applied electronic signatures thereto using their certificates;
  2. Submitting the electronic document with the electronic signatures of all other joint submitters included therein by the registered user who first submits the document;
  3. Submitting the electronic document together with an electronic conversion of a confirmation bearing the signatures or seals of all other joint submitters.
  (3) In cases of submitting an electronic document granted authentication under Chapter V-2 of the Notary Public Act, the purport thereof and matters regarding proof of authentication shall be stated.
  (4) Among affiliated users, an affiliated user who is designated with the authority to submit electronic documents may submit electronic documents by any of the following methods:
  1. Submitting the relevant electronic document by affixing an electronic signature using the certificate of the registered user who designated the affiliated user to the relevant electronic document;
  2. Converting a power of attorney of the registered user who designated the affiliated user and a letter of confirmation on which the signature or seal of such registered user is affixed into electronic documents and submitting them together.
  (5) The head of an investigative agency, etc. shall take technical measures, such as storing a hash value (referring to a unique identification value extracted from an electronic document file; hereinafter the same shall apply) of an electronic document at the time of receipt, to verify the identity of the electronic document submitted by a registered user.

Article 10 (Confirmation of identity of electronic documents) (1) A registered user who submitted an electronic document shall request confirmation of the identity of the electronic document within 14 days from the date of submitting the electronic document under Article 9(3) of the Act.
  (2) If an investigative agency, etc. receives a request for confirmation of the identity of an electronic document from a registered user under Article 9(3) of the Act, it shall allow the registered user to confirm the identity thereof by a method of allowing him or her to inspect the electronic document using an information processing device, such as a computer.
  (3) If an investigative agency, etc. receives a request for modification of an electronic document from a registered user under Article 9(4) of the Act, it shall determine whether the electronic document submitted by the registered user is identical to the received electronic document and decide whether to modify it within 14 days from the date of receiving such request.

Article 11 (Submission of digitized documents) (1) In cases of submitting a digitized document through the criminal justice portal, a registered user shall preserve the original of the document subject to digitization (referring to paper documents or other documents, drawings, photographs, audio or video data, etc. in non-electronic form; hereinafter the same shall apply) until the relevant criminal justice procedure becomes final and conclusive.
  (2) A registered user who is requested by an investigative agency, etc. to present or submit the original of an electronic document under Article 9(5) of the Act shall comply with such request.
  (3) If a registered user does not comply with a request to present or submit the original under Article 9(5) of the Act, the investigative agency, etc. may discard the submitted digitized document. In such cases, upon request by the registered user, the investigative agency, etc. may reproduce and deliver the digitized document before discarding the submitted digitized document.

Article 12 (Submission of multimedia data) (1) A registered user may submit data in a multimedia format, such as audio or video information, through the criminal justice portal. In such cases, he or she shall submit the data in a manner required by the criminal justice portal, and state the main content, purpose of submission, and file size thereof.
  (2) If the description of the main content, purpose of submission, and file size of the multimedia data under the latter part of paragraph (1) is insufficient, the investigative agency, etc. may request the submitter to supplement such description.

Article 13 (Supplementation of submitted electronic documents) (1) If a converted and submitted electronic document is illegible or it is otherwise necessary to verify the original, an investigative agency, etc. may request the person who submitted the relevant electronic document to resubmit a legible electronic document or submit the original within a specified reasonable period.
  (2) If the person who submitted the electronic document under paragraph (1) resubmits a legible electronic document or the original, he or she shall be deemed to have submitted the legible electronic document or the original at the time the electronic document was initially submitted.
  (3) Once an electronic document submitted by a registered user using the criminal justice portal has been received, the user may not delete it or resubmit it by modifying its content.
  (4) If an investigative agency, etc. determines that an electronic document is unnecessary after hearing the opinion of the person who submitted it, the agency may discard the relevant electronic document. In such cases, upon request by the person who submitted the electronic document, the agency may reproduce and deliver the electronic document before discarding the same.

Article 14 (Request for materials related to investigation) (1) Where an investigative agency, etc. requests the submission of materials from a public office or other public or private organization under Article 199(2) of the Criminal Procedure Act, it may do so by the method of electronic service or notification.
  (2) In an electronic record case, if a public office or other public or private organization submits materials held in the form of electronic documents under Article 199(2) of the Criminal Procedure Act, it may submit them through the electronic data processing system.
  (3) If the materials submitted by a public office or other public or private organization under paragraph (2) are not electronic documents, the investigative agency, etc. may request the office or organization to transmit them after converting them into electronic documents.

CHAPTER IV PREPARATION OF ELECTRONIC DOCUMENTS BY INVESTIGATIVE AGENCIES, ETC.

Article 15 (Cases exempt from preparation of electronic documents) (1) Cases where electronic documents need not be prepared under the proviso of Article 10(1) of the Act shall be as follows:
  1. Where it is not possible to determine when a failure occurring in an electronic data processing system or an information and communications network will be restored, or where waiting for the recovery from such failure is likely to cause a significant delay in procedures;
  2. Where urgency is required or the deadline for execution is imminent when executing a warrant, such as a warrant of arrest, warrant of detention, or warrant of seizure, search, and inspection, under relevant statutes or regulations such as the Criminal Procedure Act, or where it is necessary for the smooth execution of a warrant;
  3. Where it is significantly impracticable or inappropriate to prepare documents as electronic documents, such as cases where relevant documents shall be urgently prepared or delivered on site;
  4. Where an investigative agency, etc. receives a case transfer, etc. in a form other than an electronic document from an agency other than an investigative agency, etc. and continues the investigation of such case;
  5. Where the leakage of electronic records kept and managed by an investigative agency, etc. to the outside is likely to harm the existence and safety of the State or the basic order of free democracy.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), an investigative agency, etc. shall convert documents not created in electronic form into electronic documents in any of the following cases:
  1. Where each cause under paragraph (1)1 through 3 has been resolved;
  2. Where electronic records are transmitted to a criminal justice agency.

Article 16 (Preparation of protocol of examination of criminal suspect) (1) A public official affiliated with an investigative agency, etc. may allow a suspect to inspect a protocol of examination of the criminal suspect under the Criminal Procedure Act by means of an information processing device, such as a computer.
  (2) If a suspect raises an objection, such as a request for additions, subtractions, or modifications to the protocol of examination of the criminal suspect, or states an opinion to be recorded additionally under Article 244(2) of the Criminal Procedure Act, such matters may be recorded by means of an information processing device, such as a computer, and an electronic signature may be affixed thereto.
  (3) Notwithstanding paragraph (2), if the suspect requests to record matters on a paper document, the protocol of examination of a criminal suspect shall be printed out to record the relevant contents on the paper document, and such paper document shall be converted into an electronic form and registered in the electronic data processing system.
  (4) Procedures for writing one's name and affixing a seal, or signing after video recording under Article 244-2(2) of the Criminal Procedure Act and Article 30(4) of the Act on Special Cases concerning the Punishment of Sexual Crimes shall be performed by storing the hash value of the video in the electronic data processing system after completing the video recording, and having the party confirm the same.
  (5) Paragraphs (1) through (4) shall apply mutatis mutandis to cases of preparing a protocol under Article 312(4) of the Criminal Procedure Act (including cases of investigation and preparation using an internet video conference device, etc.).

Article 17 (Preparation of protocol under the Act on Protection of Specific Crime Informants) (1) A public official affiliated with an investigative agency, etc. may prepare protocols or other documents under Article 7(1) of the Act on Protection of Specific Crime Informants (hereafter in this Article referred to as "protocols, etc.") and an identity management card for a crime informant, etc. under paragraph (3) of that Article (hereafter in this Article referred to as "identity management card for a crime informant, etc.") as electronic documents.
  (2) Where a crime informant, etc. defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Act on Protection of Specific Crime Informants is required to affix a signature or thumbprint to protocols, etc. and identity management cards for crime informants, etc. prepared as electronic documents under paragraph (1) in accordance with that Act, a public official affiliated with an investigative agency, etc. shall have him or her affix an electronic signature using a pseudonym instead of a signature or thumbprint. In such cases, the electronic signature under the pseudonym shall have the same effect as a signature of the real name.

Article 18 (Reasons for exclusion from conversion into digitized documents and registration thereof) Cases where conversion and registration may be omitted because it is significantly impracticable or inappropriate to convert a document subject to digitization into an electronic form under the proviso of Article 11(1) of the Act shall be as follows:
  1. Where it is necessary to preserve the document in its original state because its current condition is of significant importance;
  2. Where it is inappropriate to convert all or part of the relevant document subject to digitization into an electronic form and register the same, considering the size, clarity, etc. of the submitted document subject to digitization, such as books or large drawings; in such cases, some important contents may be converted into an electronic form and registered;
  3. Where it is technically impracticable to convert the same into an electronic form;
  4. Where it constitutes a trade secret of a person related to the case (referring to a trade secret defined in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Unfair Competition Prevention and Trade Secret Protection Act) and business interests are infringed or are likely to be infringed if it is leaked to the outside;
  5. Where the leakage of electronic records kept and managed by an investigative agency, etc. to the outside is likely to harm the existence and safety of the State or the basic order of free democracy.
  6. Where there is a risk of additional harm to the victim if converted into electronic form;
  7. Where a large amount of data unrelated to the case is submitted, or where data is submitted repeatedly, making it inappropriate to convert the documents subject to digitization into electronic form.

Article 19 (Procedures and methods for registering digitized documents) (1) Where a public official affiliated with an investigative agency, etc. receives documents subject to digitization, he or she shall convert them into an electronic form, affix an administrative electronic signature, and register them in the electronic data processing system.
  (2) If the head of an investigative agency, etc. converts documents subject to digitization into an electronic form and registers them in the electronic data processing system under Article 11(1) of the Act, he or she shall take technical measures, such as storing the hash value of the digitized document at the time of receipt.

Article 20 (Storage and confirmation of documents subject to digitization) (1) The retention period for documents subject to digitization under Article 12(1) of the Act shall be until the time prescribed according to the classification in the subparagraphs of Article 20(1) of the Act.
  (2) If an investigative agency, etc. receives a request to return a document subject to digitization under Article 12(2) of the Act, it shall determine whether to return the document subject to digitization within 14 days from the date of receipt of the request.
  (3) When an investigative agency, etc. provides a person who submitted a document subject to digitization an opportunity to confirm the identity between the digitized document and the document subject to digitization under Article 12(3) of the Act, it shall allow such verification by permitting inspection of the digitized document using an information processing device, such as a computer.
  (4) A person to whom a document subject to digitization has been returned from a criminal justice agency under Article 12(3) of the Act shall retain the original of the document subject to digitization until the relevant criminal proceedings become final and conclusive.

Article 21 (Destruction and correction of electronic documents) (1) If a public official affiliated with an investigative agency, etc. identifies an error in an electronic document, he or she may correct it using the electronic data processing system according to the following classifications:
  1. Where it is evident that the document has no validity due to an error, etc. in the process of electronic registration: Destruction;
  2. Where it is evident that there is a miscalculation or clerical error, etc.: Correction.
  (2) If an electronic document is destroyed or corrected under paragraph (1), the fact that the electronic document has been destroyed or corrected shall be recorded in the electronic data processing system, and the electronic document prior to such destruction or correction shall be saved and stored.
  (3) Detailed procedures and methods for destruction or correction under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be determined by the head of each investigative agency, etc.

Article 22 (Circulation of electronic documents) (1) If a criminal justice agency transmits an electronic document to another agency handling criminal justice affairs through the electronic data processing system under Article 13(1) of the Act, it shall transmit it in a format that allows text information to be searched and extracted, unless there are special circumstances.
  (2) An electronic document transmitted under paragraph (1) shall be deemed to have been received when it is electronically recorded in the electronic data processing system of the criminal justice agency.
  (3) Where an electronic document is printed through the electronic data processing system under the main clause of Article 13(2) of the Act, it shall be done by accessing the electronic data processing system and printing the electronic document stored in such system. In such cases, the head of the criminal justice agency shall take technical measures to ensure the identity of the electronic document, such as ensuring that a unique number is printed on the printed document.
  (4) Under the proviso of Article 13 (2) of the Act, an agency eligible to receive electronic documents by electronic means shall be an agency designated by the Minister of Justice and publicly announced on the criminal justice portal, among agencies equipped with systems capable of transmitting and receiving electronic documents, for which the need for circulation of electronic documents is recognized in relation to criminal justice procedures.

CHAPTER 5 ELECTRONIC SERVICE AND NOTIFICATION

Article 23 (Persons eligible to receive electronic service or notification) (1) Registered users who consent to electronic service and notification under Article 14(1)3 of the Act and are eligible to receive service or notification electronically through the electronic data processing system shall be as follows:
  1. A registered user who has consented to electronic service and notification for an individual case;
  2. A registered user who has reported as a recipient of service under Article 60 of the Criminal Procedure Act;
  3. A registered user who has consented to electronic service and notification within a specified period of up to 1 year, in anticipation of becoming a suspect, defendant, victim, complainant, accuser, defense counsel, or a person falling under any subparagraph of Article 3.
  (2) If an investigative agency, etc. serves or notifies a registered user falling under any subparagraph of paragraph (1) by an electronic document using the electronic data processing system, it shall have the same effect as serving or notifying by a paper document.

Article 24 (Methods of electronic service or notification) (1) The methods for registering an electronic document in the electronic data processing system and serving or notifying such fact under Article 14(1) and (2) of the Act shall be in accordance with the following subparagraphs:
  1. In cases under Article 14(1)1 or 2 of the Act: A method of registering the electronic document in the electronic data processing system and transmitting it to the electronic data processing system of the criminal justice agency (including cases where an official affiliated therewith receives service or notification) that is to receive the service or notification of such electronic document;
  2. In cases under Article 14(1)3 of the Act: A method of registering the electronic document in the electronic data processing system and sending a notification by means of the email and text message service entered in the criminal justice portal by the registered user who is to receive the service or notification of such electronic document (excluding the text message service if the registered user has so requested).
  (2) If electronic service or notification is to be made to a person who is arrested, detained, confined, or kept in custody in a prison, detention center, or holding cell of a national police station under Article 182 of the Civil Procedure Act applied mutatis mutandis under Article 65 of the Criminal Procedure Act, such service or notification shall be made electronically to the head of such prison, detention center, or national police station through the electronic data processing system.
  (3) Where service or notification is made electronically under Article 14(1) of the Act, it shall be deemed to have been served or notified at the time prescribed in the following classifications:
  1. In cases under Article 14(1)1 or 2 of the Act: When the relevant electronic document is electronically recorded in the electronic data processing system of the criminal justice agency;
  2. In cases under Article 14(1)3 of the Act: When the person to receive service or notification views the electronic document registered in the electronic data processing system.
  (4) A registered user who has received service or notification by the method under paragraph (1)2 may access the criminal justice portal to view or print the electronic document registered in the electronic data processing system.
  (5) Cases where it is not deemed to have been notified as it is necessary to protect the rights of a suspect or defendant, etc. in criminal justice proceedings under the proviso of Article 14(4) of the Act shall be as follows:
  1. Where the registered user lacks the capacity to discern right from wrong or make decisions;
  2. Where it is impracticable for the registered user to access the criminal justice portal due to a serious illness or injury, etc.
  (6) A paper document printed from an electronic document served or notified using the electronic data processing system shall have the same effect as the electronic document.

Article 25 (Computation of period) (1) If a failure occurs in an electronic data processing system or an information and communications network, and the period during which the person to receive the service or notification cannot confirm the electronic document exceeds 1 hour per day, that day shall not be included in the period prescribed in the main clause of Article 14(4) of the Act.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), where an investigative agency, etc. has announced in advance that the use of the electronic data processing system will be temporarily suspended for inspection under Article 36(1), and the duration of suspension is 4 hours or less, such day shall be included in the period under the main clause of Article 14(4) of the Act.

Article 26 (Service of printed documents) (1) Cases where a paper document printed from an electronic document through the electronic data processing system shall be served by methods under Articles 60 through 65 of the Criminal Procedure Act in accordance with Article 15(1)3 of the Act shall be as follows:
  1. Where a failure occurs in the electronic data processing system or information and communications network, making it impossible for the person to be served to normally access the electronic document through the electronic data processing system;
  2. Other cases where an investigative agency, etc. recognizes that service of a printed document is necessary to protect the rights and interests of the person to be served.
  (2) Where an electronic document is printed as a paper document under Article 15(1) of the Act, it shall be done by accessing the electronic data processing system and printing the electronic document stored in such system. In such cases, the head of the investigative agency, etc. shall take technical measures to ensure the identity of the electronic document, such as ensuring that a unique number is printed on the printed document.
  (3) If a public official affiliated with an investigative agency, etc. serves a document printed from an electronic document under Article 15(1) of the Act, he or she may serve it by a method determined by the Minister of Justice among the optional postal services under Article 15(3) of the Postal Service Act.

CHAPTER VI ELECTRONIC ACCESS AND TRANSMISSION

Article 27 (Access to electronic documents) (1) A registered user may inspect, reproduce, or copy an electronic document or receive it by accessing the criminal justice portal to inspect the content of the electronic document stored in the electronic data processing system and printing it in writing or downloading the relevant matters to his or her own information storage medium, etc., in any of the following cases:
  1. Where he or she inspects, reproduces, or copies electronic records in accordance with the respective provisions of the Criminal Procedure Act under Article 16(1) of the Act;
  2. Where he or she inspects or copies investigation documents, etc. under Article 69 of the Regulation on Mutual Cooperation Between Prosecutors and Judicial Police Officers and General Investigation Rules;
  3. Where he or she receives a written confirmation of deletion, destruction, or return of electronic information under the latter part of Article 42(2) of the Regulation on Mutual Cooperation Between Prosecutors and Judicial Police Officers and General Investigation Rules.
  (2) If a person other than a registered user applies for inspection, reproduction, copying, or issuance of an electronic document or electronic record under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), an investigative agency, etc. may issue the relevant electronic document or electronic record by copying it onto an information storage medium, etc.
  (3) If a person intending to inspect, reproduce, copy, or receive an electronic document or electronic record is arrested, detained, confined, or kept in custody in a prison, detention center, or holding cell of a national police station, the electronic document shall be issued as a paper document printed from the electronic data processing system, or inspection shall be allowed by the method prescribed in paragraph (4).
  (4) In any of the following cases, an investigative agency, etc. may allow inspection of an electronic document using an information processing device, such as a computer:
  1. Where a defense counsel inspects a protocol of examination of a criminal suspect under Article 243-2(4) of the Criminal Procedure Act and Article 14(1) of the Regulation on Mutual Cooperation Between Prosecutors and Judicial Police Officers and General Investigation Rules;
  2. Where a defendant inspects documents or articles relating to a case for which a prosecution has been instituted under the proviso of Article 266-3(1) of the Criminal Procedure Act.
  (5) Notwithstanding paragraph (4), where it is impracticable to inspect by means of an information processing device, such as a computer, due to illness, injury, etc., inspection may be allowed by using a paper document printed from the electronic document. In such cases, the printed document shall be retrieved after the inspection is completed.
  (6) When inspecting, reproducing, copying, or printing an electronic document, the head of an investigative agency, etc. shall take technical measures, such as comparing hash values or ensuring that a unique number is printed on the printed document, to ensure that an electronic document identical to the one stored in the electronic data processing system is inspected, reproduced, copied, or printed.
  (7) When inspecting, reproducing, copying, or printing an electronic document, the head of an investigative agency, etc. may ensure that the electronic document is inspected, reproduced, copied, or printed after taking de-identification measures to prevent the identification of a specific individual by deleting or replacing part or all of personal information.

Article 28 (Electronic transmission of case records) (1) If case records are transmitted between criminal justice agencies, it shall be done by transmitting electronic records through the electronic data processing system.
  (2) Where case records have been transmitted under paragraph (1), and it is necessary to store documents subject to digitization that have not been converted and registered under the proviso of Article 11(1) of the Act, or to store documents subject to digitization under Article 12(1) of the Act, such documents subject to digitization shall be stored by the criminal justice agency required to preserve case records in accordance with statutes or regulations.
  (3) The timing and method, etc. for transmitting documents subject to digitization stored under paragraph (2) shall be determined through consultation among the relevant criminal justice agencies.

Article 29 (Issuance of copies of warrants) If a prosecutor or judicial police officer completes execution by presenting or transmitting a warrant, writ of detention for expert examination, permit, warrant of execution of punishment, written permission, request, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "warrant, etc.") issued as an electronic document under Article 17(1) of the Act, and issues a copy thereof, he or she may do so by transmitting a file marked as a copy of the relevant warrant, etc. via email, text message service, etc., or by issuing a paper document printed with such a mark.

Article 30 (Return of warrants, etc.) Where a prosecutor or judicial police officer returns a warrant, etc. issued as an electronic document under Article 17(1) of the Act in accordance with Article 35 of the Regulation on Mutual Cooperation Between Prosecutors and Judicial Police Officers and General Investigation Rules, he or she may prepare information regarding the grounds for return, etc. under paragraph (2) of that Article as an electronic document separate from the relevant warrant, etc.

Article 31 (Issuance of inventory of seized articles) Where a prosecutor or judicial police officer issues a certificate or inventory of seized articles under Article 128 (including cases applied mutatis mutandis under Article 219 of that Act) or Article 129 (including cases applied mutatis mutandis under Article 219 of that Act) of the Criminal Procedure Act, he or she may transmit it via email or text message service, etc.

CHAPTER VII TRIAL

Article 32 (Submission of electronic documents in trial proceedings) (1) A prosecutor shall submit electronic documents or electronic records to the court's electronic litigation system regarding criminal justice procedures (hereinafter referred to as "electronic litigation system") through the electronic data processing system, except in the following cases:
  1. Where there is a failure in the electronic data processing system (including the electronic litigation system) or information and communications network;
  2. Where it is necessary to protect the privacy of a victim or prevent the occurrence of additional damage;
  3. In other cases where conversion to electronic form is technically impracticable.
  (2) If documents, etc. to be submitted to the court are created as electronic documents but fall under any subparagraph of paragraph (1), a prosecutor may submit such electronic documents or electronic records by printing them through the electronic data processing system or saving them on an information storage medium.

Article 33 (Utilization of mobile communication terminal devices) (1) In a courtroom, a prosecutor may perform duties for maintaining a prosecution, such as inspecting electronic records or identity management cards, preparing trial cards, and applying for or submitting evidence, by accessing the electronic data processing system using a computer, a mobile communication terminal device (referring to a terminal device necessary for using facilities-based telecommunications services using radio frequencies allocated under the Radio Waves Act; hereinafter the same shall apply), or other devices equipped with information processing capabilities equivalent thereto.
  (2) In a courtroom, a prosecutor may transmit electronic documents necessary for maintaining a prosecution, such as applications for evidence, evidence lists, requests for examination of witnesses, and applications for amendment of the indictment, to the electronic litigation system using a computer, a mobile communication terminal device, or other devices equipped with information processing capabilities equivalent thereto.

Article 34 (Printing of electronic documents upon direction for execution of judgment) If a prosecutor prints an electronic document under Article 19(3) of the Act, he or she shall do so by accessing the electronic data processing system and printing the stored electronic document. In such cases, he or she shall take technical measures to ensure the identity of the electronic document, such as ensuring that a unique number is printed on the printed document.

CHAPTER VIII SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 35 (Use and management of electronic documents) (1) An investigative agency, etc. shall take technical measures (including measures necessary for ensuring safety under Article 29 of the Personal Information Protection Act) to prevent forgery, alteration, damage, leakage, etc. of electronic documents recorded in the electronic data processing system.
  (2) A public official affiliated with an investigative agency, etc. may use electronic records by means of a computer, a mobile communication terminal device, or other devices equipped with information processing capabilities equivalent thereto at a place outside the office buildings of the investigative agency, etc. if it is recognized as necessary for the performance of duties such as on-site verification, telecommuting, on-site investigation, or on-site guidance and supervision. In such cases, he or she shall ensure that electronic records are not disclosed, forged, altered, damaged, or leaked without good cause.

Article 36 (Operation of electronic data processing system) (1) The head of an investigative agency, etc. may temporarily restrict the use of the electronic data processing system for a period necessary for the inspection of the electronic data processing system.
  (2) When the electronic data processing system cannot be used due to a failure in the electronic data processing system or other unavoidable circumstances, the head of an investigative agency, etc. shall post the fact of such failure, and when the failure is restored, the fact of such restoration, on the website of the investigative agency, etc. and the criminal justice portal, respectively.

Article 37 (Destruction and storage of electronic documents) (1) An investigative agency, etc. may preserve permanently electronic documents related to the treatment, detention, correction, or execution of sentences of a correctional facility or the electronic records of any of the following cases, or may delay the timing of destruction under Article 20(1) of the Act, in accordance with Article 20(2) of the Act:
  1. Cases that draw significant domestic or international social attention, such as serious disaster cases, heinous crimes, or corruption crimes;
  2. Cases where an investigation into accomplices is required;
  3. Cases for which a prosecutor has rendered a disposition to suspend prosecution on the condition of probationary guidance by a probation officer under Article 247 of the Criminal Procedure Act and subparagraph 3 of Article 15 of the Act on Probation;
  4. Cases in which a prosecution has been instituted for crimes committed by high-ranking public officials as defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Act on the Establishment and Operation of the Corruption Investigation Office for High-Ranking Officials, or related crimes as defined in subparagraph 4 of that Article;
  5. Cases conducted as participatory trials under the Act on Citizen Participation in Criminal Trials;
  6. Cases in which death, life imprisonment, or imprisonment with or without labor for a term of 10 years or more has been sentenced;
  7. Cases in which a security disposition has been executed;
  8. Cases in which a motion for recovery of the right to appeal or a petition for retrial is filed before the expiration of the periods prescribed in Article 20(1)1 and 2 of the Act;
  9. Cases in which a motion for recovery of the right to appeal or a petition for retrial is anticipated;
  10. Other cases determined by the head of an investigative agency, etc., as requiring permanent preservation or deferral of destruction due to grounds equivalent to those set forth in subparagraphs 1 through 9.
  (2) If electronic documents are to be permanently preserved or if the timing of their destruction is to be deferred in accordance with paragraph (1), the procedures prescribed in Article 43 of the Enforcement Decree of the Public Records Management Act shall be followed.

